---
term: Generativity gap
section: open-problems
order: 30
definition: The lack of any reliable measure of whether a corporation's range of future possibilities is growing.
seeAlso:
  - fidelity-audit
  - ecumenical-charter
  - measurement-mechanism
---
A [[fidelity audit]] measures whether members got what they asked for. It doesn't measure what nobody thought to ask for, such as new kinds of value that members recognize only after they appear.

Every stand-in measure, such as novelty, diversity, or the number of options kept open, is open to *Goodhart's law*: when a measure becomes a target, it stops being a good measure. A charter that rewards novelty gets noise.

Until this problem is solved, accountability protects what members already want and does little for possibilities that don't exist yet. The gap matters most for an [[ecumenical charter]] whose objective is the capacity to discover new value.
